Imports:
  - Types:
      - ParsedConfigs
    From: lib/promrelabel
  - Types:
      - SDConfig
    From: lib/promscrape/discovery/kubernetes

Usages:
  registry_dispatch: |
    ScrapeConfig embeds one []SDConfig-shaped slice field per discovery provider (e.g.
    ConsulSDConfigs []consul.SDConfig, KubernetesSDConfigs []kubernetes.SDConfig,
    EC2SDConfigs []ec2.SDConfig — ~22 provider fields plus native FileSDConfigs/StaticConfigs).
    Every provider except kubernetes and http implements a shared targetLabelsGetter interface
    (GetLabels(baseDir string) ([]Labels, error)), dispatched uniformly by one generic driver,
    getScrapeWorkGeneric. Kubernetes and http instead get MustStart/MustStop lifecycle calls at
    config load/unload time because they run persistent watch/poll loops rather than
    one-shot lookups. Each provider also contributes its own SDCheckInterval flag, used to give
    its scraperGroup an independent polling cadence.

Annotations: |
  This cell is the Prometheus-compatible service-discovery and scraping engine: it loads a YAML
  scrape config, drives each configured discovery provider on its own interval, turns discovered
  target label sets into fully-resolved `ScrapeWork` units (post-relabeling), and runs the actual
  periodic HTTP scrapes, pushing decoded samples onward via a caller-supplied callback.

  Use `registry_dispatch` to understand how a new discovery provider under
  lib/promscrape/discovery/ plugs into this cell — this cell never imports every provider's full
  type, only what the registry dispatch and `ScrapeConfig` struct require.

  Use `SDConfig` from Imports (kubernetes exemplar) and `ParsedConfigs` from Imports (compiled
  relabel rules attached to every `ScrapeWork`) to see the two real cross-cell dependencies of
  this cell.

---

"Init(pushData: PushDataFunc)":
  location: scraper.go
  annotations: |
    Load the configured scrape config (-promscrape.config) and start one goroutine per
    discovery-provider type, each polling getScrapeWork on its own SDCheckInterval and
    diffing against its previous target set to start/stop individual per-target scrapers.

    `pushData`: callback invoked with a WriteRequest for every batch of scraped samples —
    this is the sole boundary between this cell and whatever stores the data (in this
    repository, app/vmagent's or app/vminsert's remote-write/self-scrape push path)

    Algorithm:
    1. Load and validate the scrape config
    2. For each configured discovery-provider type, start a scraper group polling it on its own
       interval (see `registry_dispatch`)
    3. On each poll, diff the newly discovered `ScrapeWork` set against the previous one and
       start/stop individual target scrapers accordingly
    4. Each running target scraper periodically calls its own HTTP client, applies relabeling,
       and invokes `pushData`

"Stop()":
  location: scraper.go
  annotations: |
    Signal every running scraper group and target scraper to stop, and wait for them to exit.

"CheckConfig() -> err:error":
  location: scraper.go
  annotations: |
    Validate the configured scrape config file without starting any scraping (used by the
    -promscrape.config.dryRun startup check).

"WriteConfigData(w: io.Writer)":
  location: scraper.go
  annotations: |
    Write the raw bytes of the last successfully loaded scrape config to `w` (used by the
    /config and /api/v1/status/config debug endpoints).

"ScrapeConfig(jobName: string, kubernetesSDConfigs: []SDConfig)":
  location: config.go
  annotations: |
    One job_name entry from the scrape config file: its own relabel rules plus every
    discovery-provider block configured for that job.

    Use `SDConfig` from Imports as the representative shape of each of the ~22 provider slice
    fields this type carries (`registry_dispatch` lists the rest by name).

"ScrapeWork(scrapeURL: string, scrapeInterval: time.Duration, scrapeTimeout: time.Duration, labels: Labels, relabelConfigs: ParsedConfigs, metricRelabelConfigs: ParsedConfigs)":
  location: scrapework.go
  annotations: |
    Immutable, fully-resolved unit of scrape work: one target, post service-discovery and
    post target-relabeling, ready to be scraped on `scrapeInterval`.

    `relabelConfigs`: compiled rules already applied to produce this target's final `labels`
    `metricRelabelConfigs`: compiled rules applied to each scraped sample's own labels at scrape
    time (via `ParsedConfigs`'s own Apply method, imported from lib/promrelabel)
  methods:
    "Job() -> job:string": |
      Return this target's resolved job label value.

"WriteTargetResponse(w: HTTPResponseWriter, r: HTTPRequest) -> err:error":
  location: targetstatus.go
  annotations: |
    Serve the /target_response debug endpoint: scrape one target on demand and return its raw
    response body, bypassing the normal push pipeline.

"WriteHumanReadableTargetsStatus(w: HTTPResponseWriter, r: HTTPRequest)":
  location: targetstatus.go
  annotations: |
    Serve the human-readable /targets status page listing every known target, its health, and
    its resolved labels.

"WriteServiceDiscovery(w: HTTPResponseWriter, r: HTTPRequest)":
  location: targetstatus.go
  annotations: |
    Serve the /service-discovery endpoint: for every target, show both its pre-relabeling
    discovered labels and its post-relabeling final labels, for troubleshooting relabel rules.

"WriteAPIV1Targets(w: io.Writer, state: string, scrapePool: string)":
  location: targetstatus.go
  annotations: |
    Serve the Prometheus-API-compatible /api/v1/targets endpoint.

    `state`: filter by active/dropped/empty (both)

---

Author: Goga
CreatedAt: 26/08/26
Description: |
  Service-discovery and scraping engine. Sourced by direct reading of scraper.go, scrapework.go,
  config.go, client.go, targetstatus.go, and by grepping config.go's ScrapeConfig struct and its
  getXxxSDScrapeWork dispatch methods to confirm the real (non-reflective, static-composition)
  registry pattern described in `registry_dispatch`.
